// The guest's two byte streams.
//
// A serial console is two byte streams and a wire between them, and this
// module is the wire: the board's 8250 pushes what the guest transmitted and
// pops what the keyboard typed, and the keyboard sprite fills one end. They
// are `@scratch_global` rather than plain module globals because a check reads
// them by name -- a plain `pub var` list is an arena run inside `_heap`, and a
// check that has to know the compiler's run layout to read the console is a
// check about the compiler rather than about the machine.
//
// There is only one stream now. The guest's *output* used to be drawn on the
// Stage by a terminal sprite, which drained a window of what the 8250 had
// transmitted and turned it into glyphs; the Stage is the graphics card's
// scanout and nothing else, so what is left is the record below. Nothing draws
// it, and nothing needs to: it is what a serial terminal on the board's
// connector would have shown, and a check is the reader that wants it.

/// What the keyboard has typed and the guest has not read. One queue, because
/// a board has one keyboard: the 8250's data register takes the oldest byte off
/// the front, and so does the graphics card's `KBD_DATA`. The line status
/// register only says whether there is one, and so does `KBD_STATUS`.
///
/// The queue is *bounded* and the keyboard pushes into it through `io_push`,
/// which refuses when it is full. A guest that never reads its keyboard -- a
/// Linux with no driver for the card, say -- therefore costs a fixed number of
/// items rather than a list that grows until Scratch refuses to add to it. It
/// also back-pressures the card's held keys: see `io_input_free` below.
@scratch_global
pub var input_buffer: list<num> = [];

/// How many bytes may wait. It is small because a queue that fills up is a
/// queue whose bytes are already stale: the guest is not reading as fast as the
/// keyboard is typing, and dropping the newest report is better than delivering
/// a key a second after it was released.
pub const INPUT_LIMIT: num = 32;

/// Everything the guest has ever transmitted, which is what a serial terminal
/// on the board's connector would have shown from power-on. It is also the
/// only way a check can read the guest's own output, because the *picture* is
/// pixels and there is no text in it to grep.
///
/// It is bounded because Scratch refuses to `add to list` at or past two
/// hundred thousand items. A guest that prints more than this is a guest in a
/// loop, and losing the tail of a runaway log is better than throwing
/// somewhere the machine cannot catch it. The bound is high rather than low
/// because a full-screen guest paints its screen with escape sequences: one
/// 320 by 200 frame is a hundred kilobytes of them, so a console that stopped
/// recording after the boot log would stop exactly when the picture starts.
@scratch_global
pub var console_trace: list<num> = [];

pub const TRACE_LIMIT: num = 190000;

/// One byte out of the guest, into the record and nowhere else.
pub proc io_emit(p_code: num) warp {
    if console_trace.len() < TRACE_LIMIT { console_trace.push(p_code % 256); }
}

/// Whether the keyboard has a byte waiting.
pub proc io_has_input() -> num warp {
    if input_buffer.len() > 0 { return 1; }
    return 0;
}

/// Whether the guest has read everything the keyboard has typed, which is the
/// gate a *held* key is reported through. The card's keyboard reports an edge,
/// not a press and a release -- the guest's driver holds the key down for a few
/// tics of its own after one report -- so a key held on the Stage has to be
/// reported again and again for as long as it is held. This is what says when:
/// not on a timer, but as soon as the guest has taken the last one, so the
/// guest sees a key that stays down and the queue never holds a backlog of
/// reports for a key that was released a second ago.
pub proc io_input_free() -> num warp {
    if input_buffer.len() > 0 { return 0; }
    return 1;
}

/// One byte the keyboard typed, into the queue. Refused when the queue is
/// full, which is the whole of the bound: the guest is behind, and the newest
/// report is the one to drop.
pub proc io_push(p_code: num) warp {
    if input_buffer.len() >= INPUT_LIMIT { return; }
    input_buffer.push(p_code % 256);
}

/// The oldest byte the keyboard typed, and it is consumed. Zero when nothing
/// waits, which is what the reference answers with.
pub proc io_take_input() -> num warp {
    if input_buffer.len() == 0 { return 0; }
    let byte: num = input_buffer[1];
    input_buffer.remove(1);
    return byte;
}

pub proc io_reset() warp {
    input_buffer.clear();
    console_trace.clear();
}
